Chapter: RaytraceOnScreen
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Game chapter Raytracer
  cites Game chapter Rasterizer
  cites Game chapter Color
  cites Game chapter Bresenham
  cites Math chapter Geometry
  cites Math chapter Quaternion
  cites Math chapter Cordic

 codex/test/raytracer-test's scene, a shiny red sphere and a matte green one
 over a grey floor under raytracer-test's light, traced by Raytracer into a
 Framebuf of its own and copied onto the screen that UEFI's GOP protocol
 describes, each traced pixel a block of the screen's. The red sphere rises
 and falls by the clock the framebuffer platform writes before each frame.

 `rt-shade` adds the ambient, 200 in thousandths, to diffuse and specular terms
 it divides by 1000. For a sphere's unit normal those terms reach at most one,
 so a sphere is its material's colour at a fifth of its brightness wherever the
 light stands; raytracer-test's verdict pins that colour, rgb(51, 0, 0) for the
 red sphere. The floor's normal is 1000 long, so its terms are a thousand times
 larger and the floor is its full grey.

 We say:

Section: Screen

  fb-base : Integer = 3204448256
  cell-width : Integer = 1988
  cell-height : Integer = 1992
  cell-stride : Integer = 2016
  cell-clock : Integer = 2032

  trace-w : Integer = 160
  trace-h : Integer = 120

Section: Scene

 The red sphere's centre rises and falls 600 about raytracer-test's, a full
 cycle every 6,283 milliseconds; Cordic's sine is thousandths.

  scene-at : Integer -> RtScene
  scene-at (t) =
    let bob = real-from-int (600 * cordic-sin t / 1000)
    in let s1 = ObjSphere (RtSphere { rt-center = vec3-new 0.0 bob 5000.0, rt-radius = 1000.0, rt-mat = mat-shiny rgb-red })
    in let s2 = ObjSphere (RtSphere { rt-center = vec3-new 2000.0 0.0 6000.0, rt-radius = 1500.0, rt-mat = mat-matte rgb-green })
    in let floor = ObjPlane (RtPlane { rt-point = vec3-new 0.0 (0.0 - 1000.0) 0.0, rt-normal = vec3-new 0.0 1000.0 0.0, rt-mat = mat-matte (rgb 128 128 128) })
    in let light = vec3-new (0.0 - 3000.0) 5000.0 2000.0
    in let sc = scene-new light rgb-white 200 (rgb 32 32 64)
    in scene-add (scene-add (scene-add sc s1) s2) floor

 Raytracer turns a pixel into a ray whose sideways reach, against a forward
 reach of 1, runs from minus to plus half the camera's field of view. A field
 of 1 sees about 67 degrees across; raytracer-test's camera of 1000 sees nearly
 half the sphere of directions, so at this size each sphere falls between its
 rays.

  camera : RtCamera = rt-camera-new (vec3-new 0.0 0.0 0.0) 1

Section: Copy

 Each screen pixel takes the traced pixel it falls in, so the trace fills the
 screen whatever its size.

  copy-cols : Framebuf, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  copy-cols (src) (stride) (w) (h) (y) (x) =
    if x >= w then 0
    else let c = fb-get src (x * (src.fb-width) / w) (y * (src.fb-height) / h)
    in let p = poke-32 fb-base ((y * stride + x) * 4) c
    in copy-cols src stride w h y (x + 1)

  copy-rows : Framebuf, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  copy-rows (src) (stride) (w) (h) (y) =
    if y >= h then 0
    else let d = copy-cols src stride w h y 0
    in copy-rows src stride w h (y + 1)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let w = peek-32 cell-width 0
    in let h = peek-32 cell-height 0
    in let stride = peek-32 cell-stride 0
    in let t = peek-32 cell-clock 0
    in let traced = rt-render (scene-at t) camera trace-w trace-h
    in let d = copy-rows traced stride w h 0
    in act
      print-line-uni ("clock : " & show t & " ms")
    end
  end
